{"text": "Lilly’s latest net margin is higher, but the desk could not separate that expansion into gross, operating, or net-margin series for all three lines because it would not build quarterly gross-margin or operating-margin series from LLY’s filed lines. The only quarterly margin series it returned was net margin, which rose from 21.2% [f_c5c21e5fa6ac] on 2023-06-30 to 37.4% [f_c5c21e5fa6ac] on 2026-03-31, with a sharp dip at 2023-09-30 and then a clear upward run from 2024-03-31 onward. That tells you the margin picture improved materially, but not by itself whether the driver was pricing, mix, or cost.

On management’s own explanation, Item 7 says gross margin as a percent of revenue in 2025 increased 1.7 percentage points compared with 2024, “primarily driven by favorable product mix and improved cost of production, partially offset by lower realized prices” [f_3b9843164d7a]. So the filing points to mix and cost as the main supports, with pricing a drag rather than the source of the expansion. Net income and earnings per share also increased “primarily due to higher gross margin” [f_ef153bdbd02a], which is consistent with that reading.

