{"text": "Apple’s latest 10-K says its biggest business risks are broad macroeconomic and industry risks: dependence on global and regional economic conditions, geopolitical and trade disruptions, concentrated and fragile supply chains, intense competition and rapid technological change, and the related risk that the company may not be able to protect its intellectual property or keep up with product and service innovation [r_0796f19e44b4].

On concentration, the desk’s record confirms that Apple says sales outside the U.S. are a majority of total net sales, and that its supply chain and manufacturing are also highly concentrated outside the U.S. [r_0796f19e44b4]. The record also shows Apple’s disclosure that trade receivables can be concentrated with cellular carriers and other resellers, and that vendor non-trade receivables were concentrated among a few vendors primarily in Asia [r_b1beac64eada]. I do not yet have the product-by-product revenue table or the geography-by-geography revenue table as quoted rows, so I cannot state the product or regional revenue shares as figures from this turn.

As to whether the desk holds any of this as figures: the desk does not hold the risk-factor text as figures; it holds those as quoted filing passages [r_0796f19e44b4]. The concentration disclosures currently on the record are likewise quoted text about concentration, not a quantified product or geography revenue breakdown [r_b1beac64eada]. If you want, I can ask for the Item 8 tables next and then state the product and geography mix precisely.", "problems": [{"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}]}

{"text": "The issuer side is settled, but the market side still is not. The desk can say the pretax-to-net-income gap was $12.06B [f_1de714d42617] in 2023, $16.61B [f_1de714d42617] in 2024, and $15.55B [f_1de714d42617] in 2025, so the gap widened in 2024 and then narrowed slightly in 2025. It can also say interest expense was $5.55B [f_90c1abfef32e], $26.10B [f_90c1abfef32e], and $81.32B [f_90c1abfef32e] for 2021-12-31, 2022-12-31, and 2023-12-31.

For a bank, the desk does not apply EBIT, EBITDA, free cash flow, total debt, net debt, EBIT / interest coverage, debt / EBITDA, debt / cash from operations, free cash flow / debt, current ratio, gross margin, operating margin, net margin, days sales outstanding, days inventory, days payable, NOPAT, invested capital, ROIC, quick assets, quick ratio, free cash flow margin, capex intensity, net debt / EBITDA, or cash conversion cycle because each is marked not for a financial issuer; asset turnover is also not applied because JPM is missing revenue for that measure [f_44d12c89cf06].

What still prevents the main question from being answered is the market comparison itself. The desk did not return a year-ago 30-session volatility reading for JPM, and it refused the year-ago beta-to-SPY and distance-from-52-week-high lines on check grounds, so there is no settled row to show whether JPM was being treated as riskier than a year ago."}

{"text": "Apple’s days sales outstanding over the last twelve quarters was 21.75 [f_7815daab073d], 30.00 [f_7815daab073d], 17.65 [f_7815daab073d], 21.90 [f_7815daab073d], 24.18 [f_7815daab073d], 32.03 [f_7815daab073d], 21.70 [f_7815daab073d], 24.94 [f_7815daab073d], 26.67 [f_7815daab073d], 35.33 [f_7815daab073d], 25.27 [f_7815daab073d], and 24.83 [f_7815daab073d] days.

I could not settle days inventory, days payable, or cash conversion cycle from the record shown here, and I also could not verify whether the latest 10-K changed the supplier-financing wording versus the prior 10-K. The desk’s record says those items were unresolved or refused, and for cash conversion cycle it explicitly says the desk does not hold that measure for AAPL.

On your range question, the cycle itself is not available on this desk, so I cannot place the latest reading against a three-year low and high without building the cycle from the filed components first. If you want, I can ask for the filed inventory, payable, and revenue/receivable lines needed to construct the cycle and then compare it across the last twelve quarters.", "problems": [{"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}]}

{"text": "I can’t yet answer the cross-issuer comparison, because the issuer work for ROE and equity multiplier came back refused and the book risk work says the desk does not currently hold issuer exposure rows for Bank of America or Goldman Sachs to rank leverage today.

What I can say today is limited to JPM: the desk already has a JPM issuer exposure figure on the book, but it does not have matching BAC and GS issuer figures on this run, so “is JPM the most levered of the three” is still unsettled from desk data.

To finish this properly, I need the issuer analyst to prepare the three-year ROE and equity-multiplier series for JPM, BAC and GS, and I need matching issuer exposure figures for BAC and GS if you want a desk-based leverage ranking today.", "problems": [{"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}]}

{"text": "The desk could not settle the book-wide liquidity ranking you asked for. It got stopped before it finished the full set of 20-day dollar ADV and days-to-liquidate readings for every holding, so it cannot say which names take longest from the incomplete record. It also could not compare those names with issuer-limit proximity, because the run did not expose the limit labels and room values in a usable form.

What the record does show is an execution failure, not a clean liquidity conclusion: the book liquidity question remains unresolved on the desk’s own boundary [f_9cdced3e2e93], and the issuer-limit comparison is blocked by the missing limit fields [f_962b21d11a12]. So the implication for this book is simple: there is no desk-validated answer yet on whether liquidity risk is concentrated in the same names that are closest to issuer limits.

If you want, I can try a narrower follow-up that only asks for the already-measured names the desk did return, or I can ask the risk analyst specifically for the missing issuer-limit room values if they exist in another form.", "problems": [{"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}, {"reason": "requirement_unaddressed"}]}

